    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 5\n    nc = 11\n    facility_coords = [(3, 96), (94, 6), (95, 70), (13, 62), (51, 50)]\n    customer_coords = [(53, 63), (39, 4), (28, 79), (9, 57), (20, 47), (65, 96), (78, 92), (79, 71), (37, 63), (94, 59), (25, 52)]\n    capacities = [173.0, 121.0, 119.0, 152.0, 168.0]\n    demands = [35.0, 47.0, 20.0, 39.0, 38.0, 26.0, 16.0, 22.0, 11.0, 36.0, 44.0]\n    fixed_costs = [411.0, 420.0, 293.0, 241.0, 487.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: no facility serves > 59% of total demand\n    total_demand = 334.0\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= 0.59*total_demand)\n\n    # Policy: opening budget cap $1014\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 1014.0)\n\n    # Policy: F0 and F4 cannot both open\n    m.addConstr(open_f[0] + open_f[4] <= 1)\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Network Design Decision for Warehouse Openings and Customer Service Plan\n\nPurpose\nWe are preparing to decide which distribution centers to activate from a set of five candidate locations and how to route shipments to eleven customer locations. The goal is to meet all customer demand at the lowest total cost while adhering to our budget, capacity limits, and operating policies.\n\nDecision to be Made\n- Which candidate warehouses to open.\n- How much each open warehouse should ship to each customer.\n- Confirmation that the plan meets service, budget, capacity, and compliance requirements.\n\nBusiness Requirements\n- Service coverage: Each customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied in the planning horizon. Customers may be served by one or more open sites, as needed to meet demand and optimize cost.\n- Facility activation: A site may only ship product if it is opened. Opening any site incurs a fixed activation cost that must be considered in total cost.\n- Budget discipline: The total capital outlay for warehouse activation must not exceed $1014 across all sites selected for opening. This is a hard cap and includes only the fixed activation costs.\n- Capacity adherence: Each warehouse has a defined throughput limit. Outbound volume assigned to any site must not exceed its available capacity.\n- Costing approach: The total network cost will include (a) the fixed costs to open selected sites and (b) transportation spending based on distance and volume at the standard per\u2011unit, per\u2011kilometer transport rate specified in the annex.\n- Risk diversification: To avoid over\u2011reliance on any single location, no distribution center may fulfill more than 59% of the total system demand.\n- Regulatory compliance: Due to overlapping market zones, the facilities at F0 (3, 96) and F4 (51, 50) may not be operated at the same time. The design may open either of these locations individually, or neither, but not both together.\n\nApproach\n- Validate the facility and customer data, including coordinates, capacities, demands, and fixed opening costs.\n- Evaluate feasible activation combinations that respect the capital budget and regulatory restriction on F0 and F4.\n- For each feasible activation set, allocate customer shipments from open sites so that:\n  - Every customer\u2019s full demand is met.\n  - No site\u2019s assigned volume exceeds its capacity.\n  - No site supplies more than 59% of total system demand.\n- Calculate total network cost (activation plus transportation, using the rate in the annex) for each feasible plan and identify the plan with the lowest total cost.\n- Provide a clear recommendation detailing which warehouses to open, the shipment plan to each customer, capacity utilization by site, budget usage, and expected transportation spend.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended set of warehouses to open and rationale.\n- Customer\u2011to\u2011warehouse shipment plan with volumes.\n- Summary of total landed cost (activation and transportation) for the recommended plan and key alternatives.\n- Confirmation of policy adherence: budget cap, capacity limits, 59% supply share limit, and F0/F4 operating restriction.\n- Sensitivity notes outlining how the recommendation might change if demand, capacities, or the transport rate vary within reasonable ranges.\n\nAssumptions and Notes\n- Distances for transportation costing will be derived from the provided coordinate pairs for facilities and customers.\n- All volumes are expressed in consistent units across demands, capacities, and shipments.\n- Partial allocation of a customer\u2019s demand across multiple open sites is permitted when it reduces cost or is needed to satisfy policy and capacity requirements.\n\nDecision Criterion\nSelect the configuration of opened warehouses and customer shipment plan that minimizes total cost while fully meeting demand and honoring the budget, capacity, diversification, and regulatory policies above.\n\nAnnex: Planning Data\n- Facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (3, 96)\n  - F1: (94, 6)\n  - F2: (95, 70)\n  - F3: (13, 62)\n  - F4: (51, 50)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (53, 63)\n  - C1: (39, 4)\n  - C2: (28, 79)\n  - C3: (9, 57)\n  - C4: (20, 47)\n  - C5: (65, 96)\n  - C6: (78, 92)\n  - C7: (79, 71)\n  - C8: (37, 63)\n  - C9: (94, 59)\n  - C10: (25, 52)\n- Facility capacities: [173.0, 121.0, 119.0, 152.0, 168.0]\n- Customer demands: [35.0, 47.0, 20.0, 39.0, 38.0, 26.0, 16.0, 22.0, 11.0, 36.0, 44.0]\n- Fixed costs to open facilities: [411.0, 420.0, 293.0, 241.0, 487.0]\n- Transport rate per unit per kilometer: 0.5",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Network Design Decision for Warehouse Openings and Customer Service Plan\n\nPurpose\nWe are preparing to decide which distribution centers to activate from a set of five candidate locations and how to route shipments to eleven customer locations. The goal is to meet all customer demand at the lowest total cost while adhering to our budget, capacity limits, and operating policies.\n\nDecision to be Made\n- Which candidate warehouses to open.\n- How much each open warehouse should ship to each customer.\n- Confirmation that the plan meets service, budget, capacity, and compliance requirements.\n\nBusiness Requirements\n- Service coverage: Each customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied in the planning horizon. Customers may be served by one or more open sites, as needed to meet demand and optimize cost.\n- Facility activation: A site may only ship product if it is opened. Opening any site incurs a fixed activation cost that must be considered in total cost.\n- Budget discipline: The total capital outlay for warehouse activation must not exceed $1014 across all sites selected for opening. This is a hard cap and includes only the fixed activation costs.\n- Capacity adherence: Each warehouse has a defined throughput limit. Outbound volume assigned to any site must not exceed its available capacity.\n- Costing approach: The total network cost will include (a) the fixed costs to open selected sites and (b) transportation spending based on distance and volume at the standard per\u2011unit, per\u2011kilometer transport rate specified in the annex.\n- Risk diversification: To avoid over\u2011reliance on any single location, no distribution center may fulfill more than 59% of the total system demand.\n- Regulatory compliance: Due to overlapping market zones, the facilities at F0 (3, 96) and F4 (51, 50) may not be operated at the same time. The design may open either of these locations individually, or neither, but not both together.\n\nApproach\n- Validate the facility and customer data, including coordinates, capacities, demands, and fixed opening costs.\n- Evaluate feasible activation combinations that respect the capital budget and regulatory restriction on F0 and F4.\n- For each feasible activation set, allocate customer shipments from open sites so that:\n  - Every customer\u2019s full demand is met.\n  - No site\u2019s assigned volume exceeds its capacity.\n  - No site supplies more than 59% of total system demand.\n- Calculate total network cost (activation plus transportation, using the rate in the annex) for each feasible plan and identify the plan with the lowest total cost.\n- Provide a clear recommendation detailing which warehouses to open, the shipment plan to each customer, capacity utilization by site, budget usage, and expected transportation spend.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended set of warehouses to open and rationale.\n- Customer\u2011to\u2011warehouse shipment plan with volumes.\n- Summary of total landed cost (activation and transportation) for the recommended plan and key alternatives.\n- Confirmation of policy adherence: budget cap, capacity limits, 59% supply share limit, and F0/F4 operating restriction.\n- Sensitivity notes outlining how the recommendation might change if demand, capacities, or the transport rate vary within reasonable ranges.\n\nAssumptions and Notes\n- Distances for transportation costing will be derived from the provided coordinate pairs for facilities and customers.\n- All volumes are expressed in consistent units across demands, capacities, and shipments.\n- Partial allocation of a customer\u2019s demand across multiple open sites is permitted when it reduces cost or is needed to satisfy policy and capacity requirements.\n\nDecision Criterion\nSelect the configuration of opened warehouses and customer shipment plan that minimizes total cost while fully meeting demand and honoring the budget, capacity, diversification, and regulatory policies above.\n\nAnnex: Planning Data\n- Facility coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Facility capacities: {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demands: {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Fixed costs to open facilities: {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transport rate per unit per kilometer: {TRANSPORT_RATE}"
